To evaluate the alignment of the two chunks of text, I will use a simple string similarity metric. In this case, I will use the Levenshtein distance, which measures the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions, deletions or substitutions) required to change one word into the other.

However, since the provided chunks are not words but phrases, I will use a more general approach based on the similarity of the phrases. I will use the Jaccard similarity coefficient, which measures the similarity of two sets by dividing the size of their intersection by the size of their union.

Here is the code to calculate the Jaccard similarity coefficient:

```python
def jaccard_similarity(set1, set2):
    intersection = set1.intersection(set2)
    union = set1.union(set2)
    if len(union) == 0:
        return 0
    return len(intersection) / len(union)

def lemmatize(word):
    # This is a simplified implementation of lemmatization
    # In a real-world application, you would use a library like NLTK or spaCy
    if word.endswith('s'):
        return word[:-1]
    return word

def calculate_similarity(chunk1, chunk2):
    words1 = [lemmatize(word) for word in chunk1.split()]
    words2 = [lemmatize(word) for word in chunk2.split()]
    set1 = set(words1)
    set2 = set(words2)
    return jaccard_similarity(set1, set2)

chunk1 = "-not aligned-"
chunk2 = "in Baghdad"
similarity = calculate_similarity(chunk1, chunk2)
if similarity > 0.8:
    print(5)
elif similarity > 0.5:
    print(4)
elif similarity > 0.2:
    print(3)
elif similarity > 0.1:
    print(2)
elif similarity > 0.05:
    print(1)
else:
    print(0)
```

